[Music]  our next speaker is professor rajiv  khanna  from qimi berghofer medical research  institute australia  professor khanna has been awarded order  of australia  in 2017 and has been elected as a fellow  of australian academy of health and  medical sciences  topic of professor khanna's talk is  combining  immunodiagnostics and immunotherapy for  clinical management of  infectious complications in transplant  patients  over to you professor khanna  good morning everybody today i'm going  to speak on the topic  how we can combine immunodiagnostic and  immunotherapy for clinical management of  infectious complications  in transplant patients here are my  disclosures in particular i want to  highlight links with  atara biotherapeutics this company we  have been working with for  almost now four years four to five years  on development of the t cell therapies  for cmv and abv  infectious complications remains a  significant challenge  and there are three different stages  when these  infections cause significant mobility  and mortality in transplant patients  in early phase we often see bacterial  infections  and then we see the middle period the  late period which is going up to six  months or beyond  you start seeing reactivation of these  viral infections these are persistent  latent viral infection particularly i  want to highlight the  infections like cytomegalovirus  epstein-barr virus or varicella  zostovirus  viruses often arise from the recipient  himself either the latent infection  getting reactivation  or you could get actually infection  being transmitted through the  transplanted organ or stem cells  bringing these infections  it is very critical to identify what the  source of these infections  are what are the strategies used  for the treatment of these or con  prevention of these viral reactivations  every clinical center seems to have a  different strategy and  the the traditional way is to treat all  patients that is  to give them antiviral prophylaxis from  day one  and continue up to maybe up to three to  six months or even longer than  six months up to nine months in the  heartland transplantation  there are other options available as  well for example the royal brisbane  hospital in brisbane  uses a preemptive strategy what do you  mean by preemptive is that the patient  is continuously monitored for  any viral reactivation particularly in  this case cmb or abv  and if there isn't any evidence of  certain level of viral reactivation  the patient is only given treatment if  there is a detectable virus  the other strategy is to use a  combination of both  preemptive and prophylactic therapy and  whereas the patient could initially be  treated using preemptive strategy  or given early prophylaxis and then  preemptive strategies so you give him a  set period of reflexes  and then using the whether there is any  reactivation of the virus  you give the pre-emptive therapy however  the long-term use of these via  these anti-viral drugs do cause  significant problems and in particularly  the drug resistance remains a  significant challenge  the other problems are the toxicity and  the cost of these these drugs  and it's been reported in the literature  that the long-term use of these viruses  and drugs  causes late viral diseases or rear viral  reactivation  and approximately 20 percent of these  patients should show  viral reactivation or the late viral  reactivation and the mortality and the  mobility in these patients is extremely  high  and could be up to 40 to 50 percent  there has been number of attempts made  to either predict or identify patients  who are at the high risk of developing  these viral reinfection  um we have been sort of been interested  in this area for many years and have  tried to develop an immunological sort  of a strategy of monitoring  to to to to monitor the infectious  complication in this  in the transplantation in collaboration  with  an australian company celestis inc which  is now owned by coijan we actually have  developed  a simple whole blood test which is now  marketed in europe and  and in australia and also in the u.s um  which is definitely as quantiferon cmb  this quantifying cmv kit  includes three tubes uh one tube is nil  that is  anything and the other tube has another  second tube has the  the cmb peptides and third tube has  mitogen  and the myogen gives you global t cell  stimulation where the cmb  antigen gives the activation of the t  cells so these cells  the blood is drawn into these vacutainer  tubes and  up to one million each tube and then you  incubate this tube for overnight at 37  degrees  and then you measure interference gamma  in the plasma and this actually a  graphic representation shows you how to  predict or how to analyze the  quantiferon read  results if the patient shows  in different gamma levels after  stimulation with cmv peptides above 0.2  international units  it's considered to be a safe level that  there is a robust  cmb specific t cell response and we  don't need to worry about this  if the patient choose below 0.2  international units  it's considered to be high risk the  patient is likely to have complications  associated with cmb and here is an  example how we have used this  quantifying kit to monitor the stem cell  transplant patient  here you're seeing two different um  patients  shown in the highest group and two  patients in the lowest group as a  representative  the the darker lines are shown here  are the actually the the cmb quantifier  on response the reactivity  in the test tube the dotted lines at the  viral load  and on the top you see these gray bars  are actually the antiviral drugs  as you can see this patient number on  the both high risk patients  continue to have much lower quantiferon  and seem to be losing the immunity  and the viral reactivation starts and  becomes persistent in this patient  whereas those patients at the low risk  actually who  show high level of quantifying response  show very little viral reactivation and  maintain a stable immunity  uh against the virus  we've also shown that in the same study  where when you actually have a  negative quantifier on response by day  60 which by two months approximately  you have a very high probability of cmv  reactivation up to eighty percent  whereas if your cmv posits quantify on  positive  bias by two months you have a much lower  chance of cmb reactivation so  clearly showing the acquisition of a  positive quantity front cmv test  is coincident with protection from cmb  reactivation  similarly an other group headed by  atol humar at the toronto general  hospital have also conducted  studies in kidney transplant patient  have they shown again  that if you are cmv quantiform reactive  you have a very very  low chance of getting any evidence of  cmv disease in these patients  whereas if your cmv non-reactive that is  if you don't have a reconstituted  immunity  as measured by quantifying assay you  have an increased risk of cmb active  reactivation in fact atul has again  extended this work  has now actually shown that you post  prophylaxis  you can actually measure the cmb  quantifying uh  you can measure cm immunity using  quantify technology  and if you are actually positive for cmb  reactivity that is your your t cell  immunity is normal and have you  reconstitute  you actually don't need to these give  any further treatment and it can remain  cmb free if you're non-reactive and if  you don't treat these patients  they have a very high chance of getting  cmv reactivation as shown in the red  line here  having established the immune monitoring  as a diagnostic strategy or predictable  strategy for  identifying patients who are high risk  of  infectious complication particularly  viral infection we have taken one step  forward and tried to bring in  a new form of clinical management where  we use  adoptive t cell therapy for treatment of  cmb ebv or other viral complications in  these transplant patients  in fact and t cell therapy has now  emerged as a powerful tool to to manage  viral infection in transplantation and  number of  clinical centers and biotech industry is  rapidly  coming into this particular area and  trying to develop new strategies  using either autologous or off-the-shelf  t-cell in therapies for treatment of  viral infection transplant here is an  example of a patient  who we treated with cmb specific t cells  this was a stem cell transplant patient  who actually developed a very severe cmv  colitis  following allergenic stem cell  transplant as you can see here  this patient had a very severe bleeding  in his colon  and and you can see these brown dots  which actually indicate cmp replication  going on and this  histology clearly shows severe damage by  the viral infection  and when the patient came to us he was  having multiple diarrheal episodes  and we were able to grow  cmv specific t cells from patients own  blood and when we gave him this t cell  therapy within six months the patient  was completely  cured and showed a complete resolution  of cmb colitis  and the at the architecture of the colon  returned to the normal and there was no  detectable cmv  activation in there in the gut anymore  here is another example of a  pediatric patient who actually developed  um  cmv retinitis which is a infection in  the eye  and we actually were able to get the  immune cells from  from his donor um and in this case the  patient actually had a very high viral  load in the peripheral blood  also in in the retina and there when we  we grew the immune cells the patient  received two cycles of six  t-cell therapies uh and each of the  cycle the patient shows a dramatic  improvement in the viral load  and showed a complete resolution of cmv  retinitis  as shown in the images here we have also  treated young kids with  adenoviral infection with post stem cell  transplant here is an example where  actually we're seeing rapidly  replicating  viral load with the green line and we  gave multiple infusions of adoptive t  cell therapy and showed it come and the  patient showed a complete resolution  of adenoviral infection  we've also used them these t-cell  therapies to treat  solid organ transplantation not just  stem cells in this case the pa  this is a patient who had a lung  transplant um the underlying cystic  fibrosis  and within a few weeks of  post-transplant patients started to  develop  ebv positive lymphomas in the liver and  in this case the patient  we draw the blood and we were able to  grow the immune cells in this case ebv  specific t  cells and patients showed complete  resolution of  these lymphomas by 20 weeks clearly  showing you the  the power of these immune cells  particularly the in vitro manufactured t  cells for the treatment of multiple  viral infections  in particularly we have been very  interested in in  in treating the cmb associated  complications in  solid organ transplant patients previous  studies have shown that  long-term use of prophylaxis as i  mentioned earlier uh often leads to drug  resistance  and and once the patient develops  resistance to drugs like gonzaclovir  they have a very um high chance of  having mortality or height level of  mobility associated with this particular  drug resistance  a few years ago we started a clinical  trial or using the cmb specific t-cell  therapy  in renal transplant heart and lung and  heart transplant patients  and what we do is in this process we  manufacture the cmb t cell from that  we take the peripheral blood from the  transplant recipient  and we mix these with the cmb pep mix  which is actually a combination of  class 1 and class 2 restricted epitopes  and these cells are then cultured for a  short period with il-21  which leads to the expansion of the t  cells and then we add  what we call il-2 growth factor il-2  and this cells then rapidly expand  within two weeks  as a large number of these cells undergo  very stringent testing for microbial  test  contamination and also they go through  cellular and  potency testing as well once the cells  are fully  characterized they can then be  transferred back to the clinic  for infusions and usually we give around  one to two mil  10x7 per meter square  we have a dedicated facility at our  institute which is specifically designed  to manufacture  these cell-based therapies this is one  of the largest cell therapy  manufacturing facility in australia  we have 14 clean rooms and of each of  these covers around 2000 square meter  area  and has been fully licensed by tgaf for  for t cell therapy manufacture for  clinical trials  here is the characterization of cmbt  cell products that we have manufactured  from multiple patients  and on the left hand side corner there  you can see these t  cells when we expand they're  predominantly around 90  cells are t cells of these combination  of cd8 and cd4 t  cells you do see some nk cells in there  and very small proportion probably  almost negligible and b  cells and in the second graph from the  left  you can see there is an and the  frequency of cmb specifics is a dramatic  increase once you expand these t cells  over 14 days  and we notice that the regardless of the  transplant we could expand  t cells from all different type of all  different patients  and also it didn't matter whether the  patient was recipient was cmv negative  or positive before the transplant  we could expand comparable number of t  cells  although there was a trend of better t  cell expansion in cmb positive  recipients uh compared to cme negative  the safety profile of this t-cell  therapy absolutely brilliant you know in  fact  we've hardly seen any um t-cell therapy  associated uh  severe uh adverse event um generally  these are mild to moderate  and your patients might report nausea  malaise or fatigue  or sometimes you know they might have  additional sort of um symptoms  but generally are not necessarily suited  with t-cell therapy  quite safe to give these details in fact  this is a  quite a complicated table um not to sort  of go through each bits of it the simple  stories that we recruited around 13  patients and of those  11 patients showed clinical improvement  and  5 were complete responders that is that  we could completely resolve either their  cmb disease  or the viral load and 6 had a partial  response  and two had no response um  so this actually it was a very clear-cut  indication that  how powerful or how cmvt cell therapy  can be used for the treatment of these  in these  patients here's an example of one of  those patients it was a kidney  transplant patient and you can see this  red line  with the red dots is a viral load the  blue line  is the cmv specific t cells which are  actually making it from gamma  the black line shows the actually poly  functional t cells and clearly as the  patient  is given t cell therapy shown by the  these black arrows  and they actually show the resolution of  the viral infection  the t cell immunity improves and you can  see that as the t cell immunity improves  the viral load goes down the patient  shows a complete response  at the same time we also see the  reconstitution of what we call the cd8  positive cd57 positive cell shown in the  red line  and of course is coincident with the  reconstitution of cmv immunity  and of course we have done a bit more  complicated immunological set of  analysis which we call tcr repertoire  remodeling  and all the patients who show clinical  responses show beautiful reconstitution  of t-cell immunity but also  you get you get to see this by deep  sequencing  remodeling of the t-cell receptors the  whole repertoire changes with time  in these patients so they're clearly  showing long-term establishment of cmv  specific immunity in these  patients  in spite of these successes uh in in  these patients  there are a number of challenges and one  of the major one or few of these  challenges are  the autologous cellular therapy  manufacturing from transparent patient  is often very complicated and we do see  failures particularly specifically in  those patients who are lymphopenic  and who have been on the antiviral drugs  for too long  we actually need to develop rapid access  t cell therapy for critical ill patients  because their time period between the  and the disease onset and the time that  it takes to manufacture these tests  often is quite long  and also we have to identify precise  clinical core is successful cellular  therapy  one of the approach that we have been  collaborating with the tarot bio  therapeutics is to develop this what we  call  off-the-shelf approach what the  off-the-shelf approach is is such a  powerful one then you can actually take  immune cells from healthy donors and  take the peripheral blood mononuclear  cells from the  blood collected from healthy virus  carries like you and i  and then these cells are actually  sensitized with the antigen they're  grown in the bioreactors  and you can grow these multiple doses of  t  cells and store it down in liquid  nitrogen  and what you do is you then match these  t cells with appropriate transplant  recipient  and using a proprietary algorithm and  that cells can be shipped to the patient  within 24 to 48 hours rather than taking  two weeks or  two four weeks we can now provide these  t-cell therapies  off the shelf within 48 hours here is an  example  of a study conducted by atara bio  therapeutics in collaboration with this  memorial salon catering institute uh  here they were showing beautiful you  know  clinical responses up to 60 plus percent  um  overall survival when you're given the  ebv  specific t cells uh which had been grown  as an off-the-shelf t-cell therapy  allergenic there are a few  treatment-related serious elements  very few no infusion-related toxicities  no cytokinry syndrome and only one case  of graf versus host disease showing just  remarkable results both in hematopoietic  stem cell transplantation  and solid organ transplant the  remarkable thing is that  the overall response is pretty similar  around 60  there have been number of studies now  been published around close to 300  patients been treated  and what is more interesting is that you  can see how many crs are there their  complete response  and very few adverse events very rarely  the patients have been showing  any adverse effect of the t cell therapy  so just imagine these are the t  cells which have been expanded from  healthy volunteers  and have no relationship to the patient  and use the third-party t-cells and  they're extremely safe and give a  beautiful clinical response  just to summarize um i think the  long-term  what we're going to move away from a  tolerance to off-the-shelf t-cell  therapies and here are my  acknowledgements  my colleagues at the cuyama berghofer  medical research institute particularly  corey smith leonie begley  aishna panickers virahan gary brooks and  linda jones and many others  who have contributed to this many years  of work  of developing these t-cell therapies for  various infectious complications  and my clinical colleagues at royal  adelaide hospital princess alexander  hospital in brisbane  prince charles hospital and royal  brisbane hospital again in brisbane and  the  staff at the kyogen cell therapeutics  where we actually manufacture our  t-cells  thank you very much thank you professor  khanna  for the great presentation from this  morning we had  five speakers in this theme and we have  learned a lot of different innovative  approaches from them  let's take a small break and join again  for live q and a shortly  thank you  [Music]  this is life  [Music]  a moment of joy  a dream come true all pure love  a tiny breakthrough  or maybe a triumph a constant  struggle a clash of ideas  we can make a difference create the  new the unexpected  [Music]  leading us to feel great strong  or simply human  consta